Learnning lessons:

when disko-install fails it does not clean up the zpool it created
It seems not to when it succeeds, either
on success, it does not keep the datasets mounted, and /mnt/disko-install-root does go away (stays when failed)
mount things with `sudo zfs mount -a`

Because /mnt/disko-install-root is owned and grouped by root, you need `sudo rsync -e "sudo -u localuser ssh"`
In my particular case with `/var/lib` I also needed `--rsync-path="sudo rsync"`
The full command I ran:
sudo rsync -e "sudo -u james ssh" -aAXUHvP --rsync-path="sudo rsync" paulson:/var/lib/ /mnt/disko-install-root/var/lib/
Then on paulson `sudo systemctll isolate maintenance.target`
sudo rsync -e "sudo -u james ssh" -aAXUHvP --delete --rsync-path="sudo rsync" paulson:/var/lib/ /mnt/disko-install-root/var/lib/
sudo zfs unmount -a (only do this if these are your only zfs mounts! Would be bad to do this on paulson prior to this!)


Loop back device:
`truncate -s 50G /tmp/disk.img`
`sudo losetup --find --show /tmp/disk.img` # creates loopback device and shows /dev/path to it
`losetup -a` # to see active loopback devices
`losetup -d /dev/loop0`



